-  "$comment": "🧠️ The graph memo: the slice a graph planner child produced once, kept beside the change journal and served again to the next identical request while the journal proves that nothing the planned generation read has changed. `relevance` is the closed table of what a fact row reads: `path` rows read the entry they name, `none` rows read nothing of the tree, `link` rows follow symbolic links, `resolve` rows resolve a request, `text` rows name their inputs inside their key. A kind this table does not name makes a generation unsealable (fail closed); a law keeps the table equal to the kinds the observation and generation schemas declare. Stamp refinement: the discovery frontier of a generation holds, per directory, the entry stamp (mtime, inode) captured with it; the planner itself treats a directory with an unmoved stamp (and not touched within the racy window of the capture) as one whose names, node types and absences cannot have moved (`stableDirectoriesV1`). A directory of the frontier whose facts are only entries and listings of kinds `Policy.stamped` is STAMPED: a row that adds, removes or renames a name directly inside it is relevant, a row that only says 'modified' (a content edit of a sibling no fact read; the kind is advisory) is settled by one lstat against the stamp kept in the table `.lst`. Directories a resolution or derived fact names, directories read by a kind of `Policy.following` (it follows symbolic links), and directories without a usable stamp are STRICT: every row directly inside is relevant (except the modification of a directory). `carry`: a sealed slice whose generation pointer moved is served under the newer generation when every generation-wide digest named in `shared` and the row of every owner the slice covers are identical in both manifests (the planner would verify the same facts and assemble the same projects from the same rows); otherwise the reason `pointer` names the first difference. Edit refinement: when the journal names a relevant change, the memo does not give up at once. The edit index of the sealed sidecar maps every entry to the rows that name it and every directory to the rows that read its children; the lookup re-checks exactly those rows with the planner's own checks (an analysis is replayed and compared by its result, so an edit that keeps a file's imports and modules holds), proves a directory that has relevant entries below it to be the same directory (frontier inode), lists a frontier directory whose children changed again with the planner's own walk predicates and compares its subdirectories and members, refuses a touched path that became a symbolic link, a row it cannot check (a derived fact, a closure) and more than `maximumEditPaths` paths or `maximumEditRows` rows; when everything holds it serves and re-seals the stamps of the directories it listed again.",
